   "title_cn": "#ትሰማ 运动揭示埃塞俄比亚文化权力之争",
   "title_original": "#ትሰማ Unmasks a Power Struggle Over Culture in Ethiopia",
   "summary_cn": "埃塞俄比亚一场反对性别暴力的运动#ትሰማ（意为“让她被听见”）引发更广泛的文化辩论。该运动关注针对妇女和女童的暴力案件，呼吁政府将其视为国家危机并推动机构问责。批评者则担忧该运动与受西方社会规范影响的文化变革相关。",
   "summary_original": "A campaign against gender violence ignites a wider cultural debate. The post #ትሰማ Unmasks a Power Struggle Over Culture in Ethiopia appeared first on Ethiopia Insight .",
   "event_time": "2026-09-02T02:22:15Z",
   "published_time": "2026-09-02T02:22:15Z",
   "location": "",
   "china_related": false,
   "verification_level": "single_source",
   "verification_label_cn": "单一来源",
   "independent_source_count": 1,
   "source_links": [
    {
     "url": "https://www.ethiopia-insight.com/2026/09/01/%e1%89%b5%e1%88%b0%e1%88%9b-unmasks-a-power-struggle-over-culture-in-ethiopia/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=%25e1%2589%25b5%25e1%2588%25b0%25e1%2588%259b-unmasks-a-power-struggle-over-culture-in-ethiopia&utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=%25e1%2589%25b5%25e1%2588%25b0%25e1%2588%259b-unmasks-a-power-struggle-over-culture-in-ethiopia",
     "source_name": "Ethiopia Insight",
     "source_group": "et",
     "language": "en"
    }
   ],
   "potential_impact": "",
   "progress": "",
   "current_policy_passed": true,
   "quality_gate_passed": true,
   "legacy_migration_preserved": false,
   "legacy_visibility": true,
   "publication_reason": "Stage 3B 真实采集",
   "body_extracted": "Viewpoint\n#ትሰማ Unmasks a Power Struggle Over Culture in Ethiopia\n1 September, 2026\nDaniel Hailu\nSUPPORT ETHIOPIA INSIGHT\nA campaign against gender violence ignites a wider cultural debate.\nThe ongoing campaign    #    ትሰማ      has emerged in Ethiopia at the intersection of two difficult questions: why does justice for women and girls so often fail, and how should Ethiopian society respond to demands for change?\nThe campaign, whose name means “Let Her Be Heard,” has grown through public attention to cases of violence against women and girls and what supporters describe as failures of investigation, accountability, and justice. Its message extends beyond individual cases, arguing that they reveal wider problems in how such violence is investigated, prosecuted, and remembered.\nThe campaign has drawn particular attention to cases including those of  Zewdu Haftu ,   Keneni Adugna  , and   Heaven Awot  . Supporters argue that these should not be treated as isolated tragedies but as evidence of a broader pattern in which public outrage is followed by weak accountability, delayed proceedings, missing evidence, or an inadequate institutional response.\n#ትሰማ therefore calls for more than awareness. It urges the Ethiopian government to recognize violence against women and girls, including femicide and sexual violence, as a national crisis requiring coordinated action, accountable leadership, public funding, measurable implementation, and greater transparency.\nMuch of the campaign’s visibility has come through social media, where supporters have shared stories, changed profile pictures, signed  petitions , and  called  for solidarity and institutional action around one demand: that women be heard before their suffering becomes another public tragedy.\nCritics , however, hear gender-justice language alongside debates about feminism, sexuality, and gender identity, and wonder whether the campaign is part of a broader cultural transformation increasingly influenced by Western social norms.\nThese reactions should not be reduced to a choice between supporting women and defending tradition. #ትሰማ brings together competing questions about justice, institutions, gender, and culture. The deeper question is what these demands reveal about power, what should change, and who should have a voice in deciding.\nMany Feminisms\nCr  itics   of #ትሰማ often speak of feminism as though it were a single, coherent ideology. Yet anyone familiar with  feminist scholarship  will encounter a wide diversity of perspectives—so wide that the term is often more accurately used in the plural, as “feminisms.” There are   Christian  ,   Islamic  ,   black  ,   liberal,     socialist,    radical , and other feminist traditions, each shaped by different intellectual, cultural, and political contexts. They differ over family, religion, sexuality, reproductive rights, and the relationship between biological sex and gender.\nSome embrace contemporary understandings of gender diversity, while others reject them. Some regard traditional family structures as sources of inequality, while others seek to preserve them while reforming unequal relationships within them. What unites these traditions is not agreement on every question, but a shared concern with the status, rights, and experiences of women.\nThese differences matter once feminism enters the wider debate around #ትሰማ. A campaign against femicide does not, by itself, establish a position on gender identity. A demand for institutional accountability does not settle questions of sexuality, family, or religion.\nWhen gender activists of the campaign do not articulate which feminist commitments they hold, they risk reproducing the same simplification they criticize in their opponents.  A recent work  edited by Meron Zeleke and colleagues may provide useful insight into how transforming gender-based violence in Ethiopia need not depend on a universal feminist framework.\nAt the same time, some activists   may unde  rstand   gender justice as requiring broader cultural reform. That is a legitimate argument, but it should be made openly and distinguished from the campaign itself, rather than attributed to #ትሰማ simply because some of its initiators hold that view. The important distinction, then, is not whether feminism and gender justice ever intersect—they clearly can— but whether commitment to one should be treated as a prerequisite for commitment to the other. It should not.\nCultural Anxiety\nThe anxiety surrounding #ትሰማ is easier to understand in context. The campaign did not emerge from an abstract debate about gender, but from cases involving women and girls who were killed, abused, or denied adequate justice. Its initial force therefore comes less from ideology than from grief, anger, and demands for accountability.\nYet its demands extend beyond individual cases. By calling on the state to treat violence against women and girls as a national crisis, #ትሰማ also calls for institutional change: clearer responsibility, adequate resources, stronger monitoring, and accountability when institutions fail.\nThis is where cultural anxiety enters. Gender is woven into family, marriage, inheritance, child-rearing, religion, authority, work, and social responsibility. Changes in how gender is understood can therefore affect relationships and expectations far beyond the immediate question of violence. Some Ethiopians may worry, as a result, that a campaign for gender justice could contribute to broader changes in family, sexuality, or social norms.\nSuch concerns should be neither dismissed nor treated as proof of anything. The broader commitments of some participants do not establish a coordinated agenda for the campaign as a whole. Cultural anxiety deserves to be heard, but cultural discomfort cannot, by itself, justify violence, discrimination, or institutional indifference.\nThe important question, then, is not whether #ትሰማ will produce cultural change, but how such change is understood and contested. That leads to a deeper question: what do Ethiopians mean when they invoke culture, and who has the authority to define it?\nWhose Culture\nCr  itics   sometimes speak as though Ethiopian culture were a fixed inheritance threatened by feminism. But culture has never been static. Marriage, education, work, family relationships, and women’s participation in public life have all changed through gradual adaptation and difficult contestation. The question is therefore not whether culture changes, but who decides what changes, and whose experience defines what should remain.\nThis matters because social arrangements do not distribute power equally. Those who benefit from—or have been socialized into—an existing order may experience greater equality as a loss of authority or stability, while those who have endured its injustices may see it as a recovery of dignity. What looks like cultural consensus may sometimes reflect who has historically been given the authority to define morality, family, respectability, and acceptable behavior.\nThis does not make every tradition oppressive, nor does reform require cultural erasure. Ethiopia does not face a choice between preserving culture unchanged and surrendering it to ideas perceived as foreign. All cultures survive by preserving some practices, adapting others, and discarding those that no longer serve the changing needs of their people. An Ethiopian woman demanding protection from violence is not necessarily rejecting her culture, just as a man rejecting domestic violence is not necessarily rejecting his ancestors. Both may simply be asking whether inherited arrangements can be made responsive to changing needs of Ethiopia society as a whole.\nReformers, however, have responsibilities too. Family, religion, community, mutual obligation, and shared moral expectations can provide belonging and stability. Reform that is perceived to dismiss these dimensions may create resistance rather than solidarity. Cultural change therefore requires part",
